{"module":"faith","voice":"female","duration":152.1,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Faith: used to oppress, used to liberate","start":0.0,"end":3.23,"words":[[0.01,0.41],[0.7,1.02],[1.02,1.18],[1.18,1.66],[1.94,2.27],[2.27,2.43],[2.43,3.07]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Religion has been on both sides of this history.","start":4.03,"end":6.79,"words":[[4.08,4.56],[4.56,4.74],[4.74,4.92],[4.92,5.04],[5.04,5.28],[5.28,5.59],[5.59,5.71],[5.71,5.89],[5.89,6.37]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The same Bible that was cut down into a “Slave Bible” carried the Exodus story that enslaved people sang about.","start":7.14,"end":14.54,"words":[[7.19,7.35],[7.35,7.67],[7.67,8.14],[8.14,8.38],[8.38,8.62],[8.62,8.86],[8.86,9.17],[9.17,9.49],[9.49,9.65],[9.65,10.04],[10.04,10.52],[10.52,10.92],[10.92,11.07],[11.07,11.63],[11.63,12.03],[12.03,12.26],[12.26,12.9],[12.9,13.29],[13.29,13.53],[13.53,14.01]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The invisible church","start":14.89,"end":16.33,"words":[[14.94,15.1],[15.1,15.84],[15.84,16.25]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Many enslavers controlled worship, but enslaved people met secretly in “hush harbors” to pray and sing their own way.","start":17.03,"end":24.21,"words":[[17.09,17.38],[17.38,18.04],[18.04,18.69],[18.69,19.13],[19.39,19.61],[19.61,20.19],[20.19,20.55],[20.55,20.77],[20.77,21.36],[21.36,21.5],[21.5,21.72],[21.72,22.09],[22.09,22.23],[22.23,22.53],[22.53,22.75],[22.75,22.96],[22.96,23.18],[23.18,23.4],[23.4,23.69]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the 1790s Richard Allen and Absalom Jones walked out of Philadelphia's St. George's Methodist Church after being pulled from their knees during prayer for sitting in the “wrong” section; Allen went on to found the African Methodist Episcopal Church in 1816.","start":24.56,"end":41.95,"words":[[24.61,24.77],[24.77,24.92],[24.92,26.09],[26.09,26.56],[26.56,26.87],[26.87,27.1],[27.1,27.65],[27.65,28.12],[28.12,28.43],[28.43,28.66],[28.66,28.82],[28.82,29.67],[29.67,30.06],[30.45,31.0],[31.0,31.62],[31.62,32.01],[32.01,32.32],[32.32,32.71],[32.71,33.02],[33.02,33.34],[33.34,33.57],[33.57,33.8],[33.8,34.35],[34.35,34.66],[34.66,34.81],[34.81,35.2],[35.2,35.36],[35.36,35.52],[35.52,35.75],[35.75,36.29],[36.57,36.88],[36.88,37.19],[37.19,37.35],[37.35,37.5],[37.5,37.89],[37.89,38.05],[38.05,38.59],[38.59,39.22],[39.22,39.92],[39.92,40.31],[40.31,40.46],[40.46,41.47]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Did spirituals carry escape codes?","start":42.85,"end":45.73,"words":[[42.88,43.11],[43.11,43.94],[43.94,44.24],[44.24,44.7],[44.7,45.15]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Spirituals spoke of crossing Jordan and reaching the promised land, and many listeners heard freedom in them.","start":46.43,"end":53.59,"words":[[46.47,47.32],[47.32,47.71],[47.71,47.87],[47.87,48.33],[48.33,48.8],[48.8,49.03],[49.03,49.49],[49.49,49.65],[49.65,50.19],[50.19,50.58],[50.85,51.08],[51.08,51.39],[51.39,51.93],[51.93,52.16],[52.16,52.63],[52.63,52.78],[52.78,53.09]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The popular claim that specific songs like “Follow the Drinking Gourd” were coded maps for the Underground Railroad is debated; historians have found little evidence that song was known before the 1920s.","start":53.94,"end":66.23,"words":[[53.98,54.12],[54.12,54.62],[54.62,54.98],[54.98,55.19],[55.19,55.76],[55.76,56.05],[56.05,56.34],[56.34,56.69],[56.69,56.84],[56.84,57.34],[57.34,57.62],[57.62,57.77],[57.77,58.2],[58.2,58.48],[58.48,58.62],[58.62,58.77],[58.77,59.48],[59.48,60.06],[60.06,60.2],[60.2,60.84],[61.09,61.81],[61.81,62.02],[62.02,62.38],[62.38,62.74],[62.74,63.24],[63.24,63.45],[63.45,63.67],[63.67,63.88],[63.88,64.17],[64.17,64.45],[64.45,64.6],[64.6,65.67]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The broader point, that faith sustained resistance, is well documented.","start":66.58,"end":71.51,"words":[[66.63,66.77],[66.77,67.12],[67.12,67.54],[67.79,68.0],[68.0,68.28],[68.28,68.84],[68.84,69.54],[69.79,69.93],[69.93,70.14],[70.14,70.98]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Faith against slavery","start":72.41,"end":74.61,"words":[[72.42,72.89],[72.89,73.59],[73.59,74.52]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Quakers in Germantown, Pennsylvania, issued one of the first protests against slavery in 1688.","start":75.31,"end":82.54,"words":[[75.31,75.8],[75.8,75.94],[75.94,76.72],[76.96,77.88],[78.13,78.41],[78.41,78.62],[78.62,78.76],[78.76,78.9],[78.9,79.18],[79.18,79.82],[79.82,80.24],[80.24,80.8],[80.8,80.94],[80.94,82.07]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Frederick Douglass drew a sharp line between “the Christianity of this land” and “the Christianity of Christ.”","start":82.89,"end":88.96,"words":[[82.93,83.48],[83.48,83.9],[83.9,84.11],[84.11,84.25],[84.25,84.45],[84.45,84.73],[84.73,85.14],[85.14,85.28],[85.28,86.11],[86.11,86.25],[86.25,86.46],[86.46,86.73],[86.73,86.94],[86.94,87.08],[87.08,87.91],[87.91,88.05],[88.05,88.53]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Nat Turner understood his rebellion as a religious calling.","start":89.31,"end":92.92,"words":[[89.36,89.57],[89.57,89.86],[89.86,90.42],[90.42,90.63],[90.63,91.19],[91.19,91.33],[91.33,91.47],[91.47,92.03],[92.03,92.45]]},{"el":"h3","text":"The movement's churches","start":93.82,"end":95.35,"words":[[93.87,94.03],[94.03,94.65],[94.65,95.19]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ministers founded the Southern Christian Leadership Conference in 1957, and churches served as meeting halls, schools and sanctuaries, which made them targets: the 1963 bombing of Birmingham's 16th Street Baptist Church killed four girls, and in 2015 a white supremacist murdered nine worshippers at Charleston's Emanuel AME Church.","start":96.05,"end":120.17,"words":[[96.1,96.74],[96.74,97.3],[97.3,97.46],[97.46,97.86],[97.86,98.5],[98.5,99.07],[99.07,99.71],[99.71,99.87],[99.87,101.32],[101.6,101.84],[101.84,102.4],[102.4,102.72],[102.72,102.88],[102.88,103.28],[103.28,103.69],[103.97,104.37],[104.37,104.61],[104.61,105.57],[105.85,106.18],[106.18,106.5],[106.5,106.74],[106.74,107.3],[107.58,107.74],[107.74,109.03],[109.03,109.43],[109.43,109.59],[109.59,110.23],[110.23,110.87],[110.87,111.28],[111.28,111.84],[111.84,112.24],[112.24,112.56],[112.56,112.72],[112.72,113.12],[113.4,113.65],[113.65,113.81],[113.81,114.77],[114.77,114.93],[114.93,115.25],[115.25,116.14],[116.14,116.54],[116.54,116.86],[116.86,117.42],[117.42,117.58],[117.58,118.3],[118.3,118.95],[118.95,119.19],[119.19,119.67]]},{"el":"s3","text":"People of other faiths marched too: Rabbi Abraham Joshua Heschel said that at Selma he felt his legs were praying.","start":120.52,"end":127.08,"words":[[120.56,120.91],[120.91,121.05],[121.05,121.26],[121.26,121.61],[121.61,121.96],[121.96,122.17],[122.42,122.77],[122.77,123.33],[123.33,123.82],[123.82,124.17],[124.17,124.38],[124.38,124.59],[124.59,124.73],[124.73,125.08],[125.08,125.22],[125.22,125.5],[125.5,125.71],[125.71,125.99],[125.99,126.13],[126.13,126.63]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Malcolm X's 1964 pilgrimage to Mecca changed how he saw race.","start":127.43,"end":132.79,"words":[[127.47,127.93],[127.93,128.32],[128.32,129.47],[129.47,130.24],[130.24,130.39],[130.39,130.7],[130.7,131.31],[131.31,131.54],[131.54,131.7],[131.7,131.85],[131.85,132.23]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Today","start":133.69,"end":134.4,"words":[[133.74,134.28]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Congregations run “Souls to the Polls” voting drives, food pantries and reentry programs, and the revived Poor People's Campaign (2018) organizes across faiths.","start":135.1,"end":146.61,"words":[[135.1,136.09],[136.09,136.32],[136.32,136.7],[136.7,136.85],[136.85,137.01],[137.01,137.39],[137.39,137.84],[137.84,138.38],[138.64,138.87],[138.87,139.4],[139.4,139.63],[139.63,140.24],[140.24,141.0],[141.27,141.5],[141.5,141.65],[141.65,142.18],[142.18,142.41],[142.41,142.87],[142.87,143.4],[143.4,144.32],[144.58,145.27],[145.27,145.65],[145.65,146.11]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Faith communities are natural hosts for teach-ins and coalitions.","start":146.96,"end":151.17,"words":[[146.97,147.25],[147.25,147.95],[147.95,148.02],[148.02,148.58],[148.58,149.0],[149.0,149.14],[149.14,149.64],[149.64,149.85],[149.85,150.62]]}]}